  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 241 PM CST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nA mid/upper level shortwave ridge is moving over the Northern Plains \nwhile a weak shortwave trough moves across the Canadian prairies. The \ngeneral trend is for a deep dry air mass to remain in place, though \nthis trough passage to the north will result in increasing \nunidirectional west-northwest flow resulting in increasing WAA in \nthe BL and mild temperatures Monday along with breezy to windy \nconditions (a few gusts around 35 mph). As higher level clouds clear \nwe should see a period of good mixing and daytime heating Monday and \nhighs should reach the upper 50s for many locations in the valley \nand potential around 60 (NBM 90th percentile). This is almost 20 \ndegrees above average for this time of November.  \n\nThe larger scale evolution of the pattern will be north-\nnorthwesterly in response to larger troughing developing in eastern \nCanada and several shortwaves and a cold front passing Tuesday and \nWednesday bring seasonably cooler temperatures back to the region \nfor the second half of the week. There should be enough organized \nforcing to bring at least chances for light rain to the region, \nhowever preceding deep dry air in place may limit coverage of \nmeasurable rainfall (virga and sprinkles may be more common). Still, \nNBM shows a 20-30% chance for up to 0.1\" rainfall during the Tuesday \nafternoon-Wednesday morning period.  \n\nThere is a strong signal in all ensembles than upper level ridging \nand anomalous heights/temperatures aloft return by late weekend and \nwe should see a return to above average temperatures by next weekend \nand early the next week. The net result will be continued dry \nconditions and a return to above average temperatures, while dry \nconditions persist into early next week. The eventual breakdown of \nthe larger scale ridge would at least set up the region for \nsouthwest flow and precipitation chances (rain) may increase at some \npoint the next week if that were to occur (beyond the current 7 day \nperiod).",
